1
00:00:01,127 --> 00:00:02,811
A fake credential is saved.

2
00:00:02,811 --> 00:00:06,492
We temporarily change the encryption key, then restore it.

3
00:00:06,492 --> 00:00:09,174
The first control recovers the credential.

4
00:00:09,174 --> 00:00:13,167
Now repeat the key change, and write one unrelated fake secret.

5
00:03:30,541 --> 00:03:32,795
The unrelated write returns success.

6
00:03:32,795 --> 00:03:37,114
Restore the original encryption key, and our old credential is gone.

7
00:03:37,114 --> 00:03:38,742
The stored value is null.

8
00:03:38,742 --> 00:03:41,621
A side character has erased the hero's lines.

9
00:03:43,465 --> 00:03:46,221
The first rotate-and-restore control succeeds.

10
00:03:46,221 --> 00:03:51,013
An unrelated write during the second rotation destroys the old encrypted value.

11
00:03:51,013 --> 00:03:53,290
Only synthetic credentials were used.

12
00:03:53,290 --> 00:03:56,405
Two fresh runs confirmed this high severity defect.
